  "slug": "mediated-amyloid-beta-oligomerization-43c2",
  "summary": "Four preprints apply structural and disorder analyses to human variants, fusion proteins, microproteins and retroelement prion-like domains.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of structural and sequence-level features to infer protein behaviour where direct experimental characterization is limited. For disease-linked CTSC missense variants in Papillon-Lefevre syndrome, predicted protein destabilization is linked to ClinVar pathogenicity labels. Testing four protein-folding tools on control, cancer-associated and fusion proteins shows that inter-tool agreement does not establish biological plausibility, a caution against treating prediction consensus as evidence. Structural and biophysical analysis of 30 human de novo microproteins reports intrinsic disorder and short linear motifs. In an evolutionary analysis, 26 prion-like domains were identified among 2,636 mobile element proteins, all within LTR retroelement Gag proteins, and phylogenetic mapping suggests an ancestral origin. The new element is the restriction of these prion-like domains to LTR retroelement Gag proteins, which links sequence-level screening to lineage history.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
